{"name":"Hibiscus Flower","hex":"#bc555e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hibiscus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flowering plant of the genus Hibiscus, especially Hibiscus syriacus, found in tropical to temperate regions, of some species used for making infusions/tea.","A cocktail made with champagne and cranberry juice."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hibiscus"},{"term":"flower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colorful, conspicuous structure associated with angiosperms, frequently scented and attracting various insects, and which may or may not be used for sexual reproduction.","A reproductive structure in angiosperms (flowering plants), often conspicuously colourful and typically including sepals, petals, and either or both stamens and/or a pistil."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put forth blooms.","To decorate with pictures of flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hibiscus","blocks":[{"text":"Hibiscus is a genus of plants in the family Malvaceae native to the tropics and subtropics. The genus is quite large, comprising 319 species. Hibiscus are renowned for their large, showy flowers and those species are commonly known simply as \"hibiscus\", or less widely known as rose mallow. The genus includes both annual and perennial herbaceous plants, as well as woody shrubs and small trees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Hibiscus_flower_TZ.jpg/330px-Hibiscus_flower_TZ.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hibiscus","wikidata":"Q157124"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower","wikidata":"Q506"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Fou_-_sea_hibiscus_%28Hibiscus_tiliaceus%29_flower_and_leaf_%28Niue%29.jpg/500px-Fou_-_sea_hibiscus_%28Hibiscus_tiliaceus%29_flower_and_leaf_%28Niue%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Fou_-_sea_hibiscus_%28Hibiscus_tiliaceus%29_flower_and_leaf_%28Niue%29.jpg/1280px-Fou_-_sea_hibiscus_%28Hibiscus_tiliaceus%29_flower_and_leaf_%28Niue%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Fou_-_sea_hibiscus_%28Hibiscus_tiliaceus%29_flower_and_leaf_%28Niue%29.jpg/500px-Fou_-_sea_hibiscus_%28Hibiscus_tiliaceus%29_flower_and_leaf_%28Niue%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hibiscus tiliaceus in flower","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFou_-_sea_hibiscus_(Hibiscus_tiliaceus)_flower_and_leaf_(Niue).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hibiscus_praeteritus_1DS-II_1-0988.jpg/500px-Hibiscus_praeteritus_1DS-II_1-0988.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hibiscus_praeteritus_1DS-II_1-0988.jpg/1280px-Hibiscus_praeteritus_1DS-II_1-0988.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_con